    "title": "pandas.Index.names — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1661.gf74cda2e6e documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.Index.names#\n\n- property Index.names[source]#\n- Get names on index. This method returns a FrozenList containing the names of the object. It’s primarily intended for internal use. \n  - Returns:\n    - FrozenList\n    - A FrozenList containing the object’s names, contains None if the object does not have a name.\n See also \n  - `Index.name`\n  - Index name as a string, or None for MultiIndex.\n Examples >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], name=\"x\") >>> idx.names FrozenList(['x']) >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], name=(\"x\", \"y\")) >>> idx.names FrozenList([('x', 'y')]) If the index does not have a name set: >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3]) >>> idx.names FrozenList([None])",
    "markdown_length": 668,
